tarred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Layott/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Layott","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Layott/evotv-app/commits/4e40b8fb57f1ac305e0bfa5bb09aaf20e396727d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-17T20:23:23Z","email":"ladilawalt@gmail.com","name":"ladilawalt"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-17T20:23:23Z","email":"ladilawalt@gmail.com","name":"ladilawalt"},"message":"Publish a built APK to the website\n\nThe website has been handing out `evotv-2026-08-16.apk` since 16 August, a\nbuild made before the FCM credentials existed, so everybody who downloaded the\napp from evotv.co got one where push can never work. It stayed stale because\nthe download URL was a build-time environment variable on the website: updating\nit meant a redeploy, so nobody did.\n\n`scripts/publish-apk.mjs` closes that. It asks the API for a presigned PUT, uploads\nthe bytes straight to Spaces, then registers the release, which the /apps page\nreads at request time.\n\nTwo things it will not do. It refuses a `-dirty` build, because an APK that\ncannot be traced to a commit should not be the one strangers install. And it\nreads the version, build number and commit out of the filename rather than from\ngit, so the row always describes the binary being uploaded even if the working\ntree has moved on since it was built. That is what the naming change was for.\n\nIt needs `EVOTV_API_BASE` and `EVOTV_ADMIN_TOKEN`, and names them both if they\nare missing rather than failing obscurely. No Spaces credentials on the build\nmachine.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014GkRDBJorYUURn4bkNe7JW","tree":{"sha":"5452bfa720239e00d8fcaa647e7debede93eb2aa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Layott/evotv-app/git/trees/5452bfa720239e00d8fcaa647e7debede93eb2aa"},"url":"https://api.github.com/r